    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1126">

                <pb facs="1150" n="1136" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg1136"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">814.00/7–744: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Long</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-07-07T17:00:00-04:00">July 7, 1944—5
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>392. The following circular telegram has been sent to the Missions in the other
                    American Republics except Argentina as a result of your telephone conversation
                    with Mr. Cabot.<anchor n="10" xml:id="d1126-f1"/>
                </p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“The Embassy in Guatemala City has been officially informed of the election
                        of General Federico Ponce as Provisional President of Guatemala. In as much
                        as this change of administration has been carried out without violence and
                        appears to be accepted in Guatemala as in legal form, the Department is
                        instructing our Ambassador to continue normal diplomatic relations with the
                        Guatemalan Government. The Department believes that in doing so it is acting
                        in accordance with the precedent established in the similar case which
                        recently arose in El Salvador in which it was eventually the unanimous view
                        of the American Republics that no consultation was required under resolution
                        no. 22 of the Committee of Political Defense.”<anchor n="11"
                            xml:id="d1126-f2"/>
                    </p>
                </quote>
                <p>You are authorized to continue normal diplomatic relations with the Guatemalan
                        Government.<anchor n="12" xml:id="d1126-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1126-f1" n="10" xml:id="d1126-f1-content">
                <p>John M. Cabot, Chief, Division of Caribbean and Central American Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1126-f2" n="11" xml:id="d1126-f2-content">
                <p>For text of Resolution XXII, see Emergency Advisory Committee for Political
                    Defense, <hi rendition="#italic">Second Annual Report</hi>, July 15,
                    1943–October 15, 1944 (Montevideo, 1944), p. 79.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1126-f3" n="12" xml:id="d1126-f3-content">
                <p>On July 8 Ambassador Long officially informed President Ponce and Foreign
                    Minister Salazar of U.S. continuance of relations with the Guatemalan
                    Government.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
